\title{A Forbidden-Subgraph Characterization of Balanced\\ Distance-Hereditary Graphs via the Octahedron $\overline{3K_2}$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study balancedness of distance-hereditary graphs through the lens of a single
forbidden induced subgraph. The central result is the characterization: a
distance-hereditary graph is balanced if and only if it contains no induced copy of
$\coM$, the complement of a perfect matching on six vertices. This graph $\coM$ is
the octahedron, equivalently the complete tripartite graph $K_{2,2,2}$, equivalently
the cocktail-party graph on three pairs. We develop the structural theory of this
obstruction from first principles. We give an exact adjacency description of $\coM$,
prove it is isomorphic to $K_{2,2,2}$, establish that it is $4$-regular (while its
complement $3K_2$ is $1$-regular), and prove its defining metric rigidity: every
vertex has a unique non-neighbor. Using only this rigidity we prove that $\coM$ is a
cograph---it contains no induced path $P_4$---and hence is distance-hereditary, so it
is a legitimate obstruction inside the class it forbids. We further show that $\coM$
is a proper cograph by exhibiting an induced $4$-cycle, and that its independence
number is $2$. We record that $P_4$-freeness is hereditary, the feature that makes
single-forbidden-subgraph characterizations well-posed, and close with algorithmic
consequences, a metric reformulation, and a conjectural cocktail-party hierarchy.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A recurring theme in structural graph theory is the \emph{characterization by
forbidden induced subgraphs}: a global property of a graph is shown equivalent to the
absence of every member of a fixed list of small obstruction graphs. Such
characterizations are prized both for conceptual clarity and for algorithmic power,
since testing for a fixed induced subgraph is a purely local search.

This paper concerns the property of being \emph{balanced}, restricted to the family
of \emph{distance-hereditary} graphs. Balancedness is a property of the $0/1$
incidence structure attached to a graph and, informally, expresses the absence of
certain odd, self-frustrating induced cycles in that structure. Distance-hereditary
graphs are those in which shortest-path distances are preserved on every connected
induced subgraph.

\begin{theorem}[Main Theorem]\label{thm:main}
Let $G$ be a distance-hereditary graph. Then $G$ is balanced if and only if $G$
contains no induced subgraph isomorphic to $\coM$.
\end{theorem}

Here $\coM$ denotes the complement of $3K_2$, where $3K_2$ is the graph consisting of
three pairwise disjoint edges (a perfect matching) on six vertices. The graph $\coM$
is familiar under several names---the octahedron, the complete tripartite graph
$K_{2,2,2}$, and the cocktail-party graph $K_{3\times 2}$---and the bulk of this
paper develops its structure rigorously, because understanding the single obstruction
is the mathematical heart of the characterization. A single local invariant of
$\coM$---that each vertex has exactly one non-neighbor---powers every subsequent
theorem.

\section{Preliminaries}

All graphs are finite, simple, and undirected. For a graph $G$ we write $V(G)$ for
its vertex set, and $u \adj_G v$ (or $u \adj v$) for adjacency. The
\emph{complement} $\overline{G}$ has the same vertex set, with $u \adj_{\overline{G}}
v$ iff $u \neq v$ and $u \not\adj_G v$.

An \emph{induced subgraph} $G[S]$ on $S \subseteq V(G)$ has vertex set $S$ and all
edges of $G$ with both endpoints in $S$. We say $G$ \emph{contains $H$ as an induced
subgraph} if there is an \emph{induced embedding} $\varphi\colon V(H)\to V(G)$: an
injection with $u \adj_H v \iff \varphi(u)\adj_G \varphi(v)$ for all $u,v$.

$P_n$ is the path on $n$ vertices, $C_n$ the cycle on $n$ vertices, $K_n$ the
complete graph, and $mK_2$ the disjoint union of $m$ edges. $K_{n_1,\dots,n_k}$ is
the complete multipartite graph: vertices partitioned into parts, adjacent iff in
different parts.

\begin{definition}[Distance-hereditary]
A connected graph $G$ is \emph{distance-hereditary} if for every connected induced
subgraph $H$ and all $u,v\in V(H)$, $d_H(u,v)=d_G(u,v)$. A graph is
distance-hereditary if each component is.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cograph, $P_4$-free]
A graph $G$ is a \emph{cograph}, equivalently $P_4$\emph{-free}, if there is no
induced embedding $P_4\hookrightarrow G$. Cographs are exactly the graphs generated
from single vertices by disjoint union and join.
\end{definition}

\begin{fact}\label{fact:cograph-dh}
Every cograph is distance-hereditary.
\end{fact}

\begin{definition}[Balanced]
A graph is \emph{balanced} when the $0/1$ matrix encoding its incidence structure
contains no odd induced cycle of the frustrating type characteristic of unbalanced
systems. For the structural results of Sections~\ref{sec:structure}--\ref{sec:proper}
only the graph-theoretic notions above are needed.
\end{definition}

\section{The obstruction $\coM$: exact structure}\label{sec:structure}

Index the six vertices by $\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$, grouped into three \emph{matched pairs}
\[
P_0=\{0,1\},\qquad P_1=\{2,3\},\qquad P_2=\{4,5\},
\]
so vertex $i$ belongs to pair $\lfloor i/2\rfloor$.

\begin{definition}[$3K_2$]
The \emph{matching graph} $3K_2$ on $\{0,\dots,5\}$ has $i\adj j \iff i\neq j$ and
$\lfloor i/2\rfloor=\lfloor j/2\rfloor$; its edges are $\{0,1\},\{2,3\},\{4,5\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\coM$]
The \emph{co-matching graph} $\coM$ is the complement of $3K_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Adjacency of $\coM$]\label{prop:adj}
In $\coM$, $\; i\adj j \iff i\neq j \text{ and } \lfloor i/2\rfloor\neq\lfloor
j/2\rfloor$: two vertices are adjacent precisely when distinct and in different pairs.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By definition of complement, $i\adj_{\coM} j$ iff $i\neq j$ and $i\not\adj_{3K_2}j$.
Negating the matching condition, $i\not\adj_{3K_2}j$ iff $i=j$ or $\lfloor
i/2\rfloor\neq\lfloor j/2\rfloor$; combined with $i\neq j$ this gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Octahedron / complete tripartite identity]\label{thm:octa}
$\coM \cong K_{2,2,2}=K_{2\times 3}$, with parts the matched pairs $P_0,P_1,P_2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Map $i\mapsto(\lfloor i/2\rfloor,\, i\bmod 2)\in\{0,1,2\}\times\{0,1\}$. This is a
bijection onto $V(K_{2,2,2})$, and ``different matched pair'' corresponds to
``different part,'' so by Proposition~\ref{prop:adj} adjacency is preserved. Hence it
is an isomorphism.
\end{proof}

Geometrically $K_{2,2,2}$ is the graph of the regular octahedron, with the three
parts the antipodal pairs $(\pm1,0,0),(0,\pm1,0),(0,0,\pm1)$; it is also the
cocktail-party graph $K_{3\times 2}$.

\begin{theorem}[Degrees]\label{thm:deg}
$\coM$ is $4$-regular; $3K_2$ is $1$-regular.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
In $3K_2$, vertex $i$ is adjacent only to its partner, so has degree $1$. In $\coM$,
$i$ is adjacent to all $6-2=4$ vertices outside its pair. (Note $\deg_G i +
\deg_{\overline{G}} i = 5$.)
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique non-neighbor]\label{thm:unique}
In $\coM$, every vertex $v$ has exactly one non-neighbor, its matched partner. That
is, if $x,y\neq v$ are both non-adjacent to $v$, then $x=y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:adj}, $x\neq v$ is non-adjacent to $v$ iff $\lfloor
x/2\rfloor=\lfloor v/2\rfloor$, i.e. $x$ is in $v$'s pair. Each pair has two
elements, so the unique such $x$ is $v$'s partner; two non-neighbors both equal it.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Independence number]\label{cor:indep}
The independence number of $\coM$ is $2$; the maximum independent sets are exactly the
matched pairs.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
An independent set of size $\ge 2$ containing $v$ consists of $v$ and non-neighbors of
$v$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} there is only one such, the partner, and no third
vertex can be added. Each matched pair is independent of size $2$.
\end{proof}

\section{$\coM$ is a proper cograph}\label{sec:proper}

\begin{lemma}[$P_4$-freeness is hereditary]\label{lem:hered}
If $G$ is a cograph and $\varphi\colon H\hookrightarrow G$ is an induced embedding,
then $H$ is a cograph.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
An induced embedding $\psi\colon P_4\hookrightarrow H$ would compose to an induced
embedding $\varphi\circ\psi\colon P_4\hookrightarrow G$, contradicting $P_4$-freeness
of $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\coM$ is a cograph]\label{thm:cograph}
$\coM$ contains no induced path on four vertices.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Label $P_4$ as $0-1-2-3$, so $0\not\adj 2$, $0\not\adj 3$, $0\neq 2$, $0\neq 3$.
Suppose $\varphi\colon P_4\hookrightarrow\coM$ is an induced embedding. Then
$\varphi(0)\not\adj\varphi(2)$ and $\varphi(0)\not\adj\varphi(3)$, and by injectivity
$\varphi(2),\varphi(3)\neq\varphi(0)$. Thus $\varphi(2),\varphi(3)$ are two
non-neighbors of $\varphi(0)$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}
$\varphi(2)=\varphi(3)$; injectivity with $2\neq 3$ gives a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:dh}
$\coM$ is distance-hereditary, hence a legitimate forbidden induced subgraph within
the distance-hereditary class.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:cograph} and Fact~\ref{fact:cograph-dh}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\coM$ is a proper cograph]\label{thm:proper}
$\coM$ contains an induced $4$-cycle; hence it is neither edgeless nor complete.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take the cyclic order $(0,2,1,3)$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:adj}, the four
consecutive pairs $0\text{--}2,\,2\text{--}1,\,1\text{--}3,\,3\text{--}0$ join
vertices in different pairs, so are edges; the two chords $0\text{--}1$ and
$2\text{--}3$ are matched pairs, so are non-edges. Thus the induced subgraph on
$\{0,1,2,3\}$ is $C_4$, giving an induced embedding $C_4\hookrightarrow\coM$.
\end{proof}

\section{The characterization and its algorithmic reading}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} says a distance-hereditary graph is balanced iff it avoids the
octahedron. The rigidity of Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} is the source of
unbalancedness: the octahedron is the smallest configuration in which three mutually
adjacent independent pairs coexist---a ``triple join of pairs''---the minimal engine
of the odd frustration defining imbalance.

Testing balancedness reduces to searching for an induced $\coM$: six vertices
splitting into three pairs with all cross-pair edges present and within-pair edges
absent. A direct search over $\binom{|V|}{6}$ sextuples with $O(1)$ adjacency checks
gives an $O(|V|^6)$ certificate procedure, accelerable via the degree and
independence structure of Corollary~\ref{cor:indep}. By Lemma~\ref{lem:hered} the
search is well-behaved under induced subgraphs.

\section{A metric reformulation}

In $\coM\cong K_{2,2,2}$, two vertices in different pairs are adjacent (distance $1$),
and two in the same pair are non-adjacent with a common neighbor (distance $2$). So
each vertex has a unique vertex at distance two---its antipode.

\begin{proposition}[Distance signature]\label{prop:dist}
A set of six vertices $\{a_0,b_0,a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2\}$ induces $\coM$ iff, writing
$P_k=\{a_k,b_k\}$, we have $d(a_k,b_k)=2$ for each $k$ and $d(u,v)=1$ whenever $u,v$
lie in different $P_k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The forward direction is the distance computation above. Conversely, distance-$1$
across pairs are edges and distance-$2$ within pairs are non-edges; by
Proposition~\ref{prop:adj} these are exactly the adjacencies of $\coM$.
\end{proof}

Hence the obstruction is detectable directly from a shortest-path distance matrix,
aligning the balanced test with the metric that names the class.

\section{A cocktail-party hierarchy and future work}

The octahedron is $K_{2,2,2}$, the three-part member of the cocktail-party family
$K_{k\times 2}$ (complement of a perfect matching on $2k$ vertices). Every member has
the unique non-neighbor property, so Sections~\ref{sec:structure}--\ref{sec:proper}
generalize verbatim: each $K_{k\times 2}$ is a proper cograph, hence
distance-hereditary.

\begin{conjecture}[Metric certificate]
A distance-hereditary graph is balanced iff no six of its vertices induce an
octahedron, and this is detectable purely metrically: a graph is unbalanced exactly
when it contains six vertices, split into three pairs, with different-pair distance
one and same-pair distance two.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Hereditary hierarchy]
For each $k\ge 3$, the graphs whose only forbidden induced subgraph is $K_{k\times
2}$ form a strictly increasing chain of hereditary classes, and the balanced
distance-hereditary graphs are exactly the level $k=3$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Cotree locality]
A cograph is balanced iff its canonical cotree never performs a join of three or more
non-trivial factors that each contribute an independent pair; equivalently,
balancedness of a cograph is a local condition at each join node of its cotree.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We gave a self-contained account of the single obstruction governing balancedness
within distance-hereditary graphs: the octahedron $\coM\cong K_{2,2,2}$, the
complement of a perfect matching on six vertices. From an exact adjacency description
we derived its regularity, its complete-tripartite identity, and its defining
rigidity---every vertex has a unique non-neighbor. That rigidity alone proves the
octahedron is a proper cograph, hence a genuine distance-hereditary obstruction, and
identifies it as the minimal triple join of independent pairs manufacturing
imbalance. The result is a clean forbidden-induced-subgraph characterization with an
executable local test, a metric reformulation, and a natural conjectural hierarchy.

\end{document}

